## Problem

I maintain `nestjs-zod`, and there's an issue there about dates: https://github.com/BenLorantfy/nestjs-zod/issues/184

Basically, consumers want to be able to provide a schema like this that describes the HTTP response for a nestjs endpoint:
```
z.object({
  myDate: z.date(),
})
```

And have it create an OpenAPI schema like this:
```
{
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    myDate: {
        type: 'string',
        format: 'date-time'
    }
  },
  required: ["myDate"]
}
```

I think this is safe to be the default behaviour for `nestjs-zod`, because `nestjs` calls `JSON.stringify()` on the response.  And when you do `JSON.stringify()` on a `Date`, it gets converted to a string:

<img width="669" height="271" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4df4cefd-a0eb-47d7-aaaa-c1a6a441ca14" />

So I'm tempted to add this override, as per the zod documentation:
```
// support z.date() as ISO datetime strings
const result = z.toJSONSchema(z.date(), {
  unrepresentable: "any",
  override: (ctx) => {
    const def = ctx.zodSchema._zod.def;
    if(def.type ==="date"){
      ctx.jsonSchema.type = "string";
      ctx.jsonSchema.format = "date-time";
    }
  },
});
```
However, this would involve setting `unrepresentable: "any"`.  I don't necessarily want to do that, because there are other things not representable in JSON that I still want to throw an error by default.  I think all of the following I would still want to throw an error (except for Date)

